WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.036
-Our next guest is
an Emmy-winning actor

00:00:03.036 --> 00:00:05.238
you know best from his portrayal
of Logan Roy on "Succession."

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:07.974
His acting tutorials are
available now on BBC Maestro.

00:00:07.974 --> 00:00:10.677
Please welcome back to the show,
our friend Brian Cox.

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:13.679
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:28.194 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:32.131
-Welcome back, Brian!
-Nice to be here, as always.

00:00:32.131 --> 00:00:34.867
-You know, I won't
spend too much time on it,

00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:36.168
but this is the first time
I've seen you

00:00:36.168 --> 00:00:38.571
since the end of the fantastic
show, "Succession."

00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:41.040
-Yeah.
-And it was a season,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.943
it ended so beautifully
and nailed the landing.

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:46.045
You had one of
the great lines --

00:00:46.045 --> 00:00:47.647
and I think Logan had many
over the years --

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:48.948
which is,
he said to his children,

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:50.817
"I love you,
but you're not serious people."

00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:52.251
-Yeah.

00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:54.553
-That really sort of summed
it all up, didn't it?

00:00:54.553 --> 00:00:57.256
-Yeah, well, actually
it was my favorite line

00:00:57.256 --> 00:00:59.091
that I had to say
throughout the whole show.

00:00:59.091 --> 00:01:01.193
-Yeah?
-I love that line.

00:01:01.193 --> 00:01:04.130
And I just thought,
"Why didn't I say it earlier?"

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:05.598
-Yeah.

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:07.200
It's true. They certainly
were pretty unserious.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.934
-Yeah, yeah. They were.

00:01:08.934 --> 00:01:12.639
They were damned unserious
most of the time.

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:14.440
-Were you --

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.775
I do feel like we've given the
world enough time to catch up,

00:01:16.775 --> 00:01:18.510
but are you surprised,

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.114
or how do you think Logan
would feel if he'd been around

00:01:21.114 --> 00:01:23.749
at the fact that Tom,
in the end, won the succession?

00:01:23.749 --> 00:01:26.052
-But I think that Logan
had already planned that.

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:27.187
-Yeah.

00:01:27.187 --> 00:01:28.521
-I think he'd
already planned that.

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:30.022
Certainly
the Skarsgard character,

00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:31.323
that it was all
going to go that way,

00:01:31.323 --> 00:01:33.125
because he could see
that those children

00:01:33.125 --> 00:01:35.061
were not going to
add up to anything.

00:01:35.061 --> 00:01:36.729
So I think that --
-[ Laughter ]

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:39.065
-Well, they didn't
add up to anything.

00:01:39.065 --> 00:01:41.734
[ Laughs ]
They added up to zilch.

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:46.105
Anyway, what I felt was
that I thought

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:47.707
that Tom was very kind to him.

00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:50.410
You know, at one point,
he had a UTI infection,

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:53.146
and Tom actually helped him.

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:55.348
So I think that, you know --

00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:57.216
And I thought, I think,

00:01:57.216 --> 00:01:59.218
"Well, you have to put up
with my horrible daughter,

00:01:59.218 --> 00:02:00.786
so I've got to give something
to reward you."

00:02:00.786 --> 00:02:02.488
[ Crowd gasps ]

00:02:02.488 --> 00:02:04.157
-It was a bad lot.
It was a bad lot.

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:05.592
Everybody's reacting like --

00:02:05.592 --> 00:02:07.660
And they should know, these
aren't Brian's real children.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.328
[ Laughter ]

00:02:09.328 --> 00:02:12.732
You know, again, for those
who haven't seen, I apologize,

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:15.467
but Logan left us.

00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:18.304
You know, he passed away
early in the season,

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:20.440
I think maybe
earlier than people thought.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:21.540
-I think a lot of people --

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.376
I had people saying,

00:02:23.376 --> 00:02:24.977
"Oh, no, I'm not going to
watch it anymore.

00:02:24.977 --> 00:02:26.345
You've gone.
I'm not going to watch it."

00:02:26.345 --> 00:02:27.947
I said,
"But it's called 'Succession.'"

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:29.215
-Yeah.

00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:30.616
-That's the whole point
of the show.

00:02:30.616 --> 00:02:33.653
You have to have the succession.
-Yeah.

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:35.054
-They kind of told you
with the title

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:37.090
where this was headed.
-Yes, it does.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:39.224
But you know,
people can be so dumb, you know?

00:02:39.224 --> 00:02:40.559
-
Yeah. -Come on.

00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:41.894
Really, get it right.

00:02:41.894 --> 00:02:44.663
-Of the three kids in the --
where are they now?

00:02:44.663 --> 00:02:46.899
Who do you think is doing
the best in the fictional world?

00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:49.535
-In the fictional world? I...

00:02:49.535 --> 00:02:52.838
Well, I think they're struggling
in the fictional world.

00:02:52.838 --> 00:02:54.673
I think they're doing very well
in the real world.

00:02:54.673 --> 00:02:57.910
I mean, Sarah is
doing a fantastic job in London.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:01.481
And Jeremy, I think,
is about to do a play here.

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:03.015
I don't know
what Kieran's up to,

00:03:03.015 --> 00:03:05.451
but he's probably up to
something extraordinary.

00:03:05.451 --> 00:03:06.652
He usually is.

00:03:06.652 --> 00:03:10.323
So yeah, I think they're
all doing probably well.

00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:13.126
It was an extraordinary
show to work on,

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:15.961
because we did really become
like a family

00:03:15.961 --> 00:03:21.233
and we were very, very close,
and it's very odd now.

00:03:21.233 --> 00:03:23.268
I kind of miss them.
-I would imagine.

00:03:23.268 --> 00:03:25.371
-Yeah, you miss them.

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:28.374
Because it was the most
extraordinary six years.

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:30.743
You know, over six years,
the whole thing took place.

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:34.680
And Jesse was just amazing --
Jesse Armstrong, who wrote it,

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.749
and the writers,
the whole thing.

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:38.484
The whole job, it's probably

00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:39.919
the best job I've ever had.

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:41.320
-Well, it was certainly

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.689
one of the best shows
I've ever seen,

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:44.857
and congratulations
to all of you.

00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:46.058
-Yeah, it was great.
It was great.

00:03:46.058 --> 00:03:48.827
-So BBC Maestro --
this is fantastic as well.

00:03:48.827 --> 00:03:51.197
I've seen clips. It seems like
a perfect fit for you.

00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:53.966
You were giving
acting tutorials, teaching.

00:03:53.966 --> 00:03:55.634
You first started
teaching acting

00:03:55.634 --> 00:03:57.336
when you were 24 years old?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:57.336 --> 00:03:58.604
-How did that first
come to pass?

00:03:58.604 --> 00:03:59.839
-Well, it happened,

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:03.276
I was doing a play
in London in the West End,

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:06.112
and the guy,
sadly he's no longer with us.

00:04:06.112 --> 00:04:07.347
Mind you, it was 50 years ago,

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:11.984
because not many people
are with us, so...

00:04:11.984 --> 00:04:17.056
Anyway, he said, "Would
you like to come and teach?"

00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:18.724
And I said, "Norman."
He was called Norman.

00:04:18.724 --> 00:04:20.392
And I said,
"Norman, I'm only 24."

00:04:20.392 --> 00:04:22.995
He said, "No, I think
it's time that you did that.

00:04:22.995 --> 00:04:25.030
I think you've got a lot.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.666
You're fresh, you're new."

00:04:27.666 --> 00:04:31.571
I was, by then, I was probably
nine years out of drama school.

00:04:31.571 --> 00:04:34.006
And I went
and I was very lucky,

00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:38.044
because in my first group that
I had was Dame Harriet Walter.

00:04:38.044 --> 00:04:39.645
-Oh, my goodness.
She's fantastic.

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:41.613
-I know, I know.
She was one of my students.

00:04:41.613 --> 00:04:43.616
-And that's who played
your ex-wife on "Succession."

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:45.051
-Who played my ex-wife
in the show,

00:04:45.051 --> 00:04:46.152
which was kind of, you know,

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:47.820
we've worked together
over the years.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.256
And then there was
another wonderful actor

00:04:50.256 --> 00:04:53.025
who sadly died of AIDS
called Ian Charleson.

00:04:53.025 --> 00:04:55.594
Now, Ian was,
I don't know if you remember

00:04:55.594 --> 00:04:57.596
"Chariots of Fire"?
-Sure, of course.

00:04:57.596 --> 00:04:58.997
-He played Eric Little.
He played the runner,

00:04:58.997 --> 00:05:01.033
and he was a wonderful,
wonderful actor

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:03.436
and a great singer.

00:05:03.436 --> 00:05:06.405
They did this big revival
of "Guys and Dolls"

00:05:06.405 --> 00:05:09.142
and he played
Sky Masterson and was superb.

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:10.243
But sadly...

00:05:10.243 --> 00:05:12.544
And he then went on
to play Hamlet,

00:05:12.544 --> 00:05:14.146
which he didn't particularly
want to do,

00:05:14.146 --> 00:05:15.782
because he was very ill.

00:05:15.782 --> 00:05:17.550
And he wondered
why he wasn't asked

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:18.918
to do it in the first place.

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:21.420
And I said, you know,
"That's the way it is, Ian."

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:23.655
But he played it
and he literally played it

00:05:23.655 --> 00:05:25.357
up until
two days before he died.

00:05:25.357 --> 00:05:26.759
-Unbelievable.
-It was astonishing,

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:27.760
absolutely astonishing.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:28.995
-And could you tell?

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:30.430
In a class like that, obviously,

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:31.964
I'm sure everyone
has a level of talent,

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:34.266
but can you always tell
when someone's special?

00:05:34.266 --> 00:05:36.669
-Oh, yeah, you can.
And there are sleepers.

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:39.605
You know, there are people
who are going to take time,

00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:41.874
but they have
the right attitude.

00:05:41.874 --> 00:05:44.242
It's just that they've got to
trust themselves.

00:05:44.242 --> 00:05:45.645
And it's all about trust.

00:05:45.645 --> 00:05:48.580
You know, it's trusting yourself
more than anything else.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:52.251
That's what makes a good actor,
is to trust who you are.

00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:55.421
And drama school is very good
for that, finding that,

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:58.357
where you can learn
that trust in yourself.

00:05:58.357 --> 00:06:00.058
That's why I believe
in drama education.

00:06:00.058 --> 00:06:02.428
I think it's a great education,
not just for drama,

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:03.962
but for everything,
for life, actually.

00:06:03.962 --> 00:06:05.331
-I also liked that --

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:07.433
not that you take it
too seriously,

00:06:07.433 --> 00:06:11.003
or I guess you don't
make it more complicated

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:12.238
than it needs to be.

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:13.705
In one of the clips I saw,
you talked about

00:06:13.705 --> 00:06:16.109
how you had trouble with lines,

00:06:16.109 --> 00:06:17.509
learning lines,
a very tricky thing,

00:06:17.509 --> 00:06:20.279
but one of your advice
for students was

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:21.347
"just learn the [bleep] lines

00:06:21.347 --> 00:06:22.782
and don't bump
into the furniture."

00:06:22.782 --> 00:06:23.850
-Yeah.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:25.284
[ Laughter ]

00:06:25.284 --> 00:06:27.086
-That's kind of
a nice one to know.

00:06:27.086 --> 00:06:29.856
-Well, that is
the basic principle of acting.

00:06:29.856 --> 00:06:31.457
-Yeah.

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:34.026
Everything after that,
it's just gravy.

00:06:34.026 --> 00:06:35.828
-It's all kind of, you know,

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:38.164
it's all bollocks,
really, after that.

00:06:38.164 --> 00:06:39.599
But don't bump
into the furniture

00:06:39.599 --> 00:06:41.634
and learn your [bleep] lines
is what you're supposed to do.

00:06:41.634 --> 00:06:42.835
-But that did not,

00:06:42.835 --> 00:06:44.170
learning lines did not
come naturally to you.

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:46.405
-No, I was not a good learner.

00:06:46.405 --> 00:06:49.174
It's the discipline
that I've had to --

00:06:49.174 --> 00:06:51.077
I mean, I'm about to do
"Long Day's Journey Into Night."

00:06:51.077 --> 00:06:52.578
-Oh, my goodness, yeah.

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:54.813
-I'm going to be doing that
in London in the new year.

00:06:54.813 --> 00:06:57.549
And I'm dreading it,
because I've got to go through

00:06:57.549 --> 00:06:58.917
the learning line process.

00:06:58.917 --> 00:07:00.352
And as you get older,

00:07:00.352 --> 00:07:04.089
the synapses are not quite
working in the same way.

00:07:04.089 --> 00:07:06.925
-I know television is different,
obviously, multiple takes

00:07:06.925 --> 00:07:08.594
than being on stage,

00:07:08.594 --> 00:07:11.297
but I would imagine
with shows like "Succession,"

00:07:11.297 --> 00:07:12.398
with a show like "Deadwood,"

00:07:12.398 --> 00:07:14.933
where they're
so beautifully written

00:07:14.933 --> 00:07:17.536
but in a way that you haven't
heard people speak before.

00:07:17.536 --> 00:07:19.705
So you might think it might
be unnatural to learn.

00:07:19.705 --> 00:07:22.574
But does beautiful writing make
the learning process easier?

00:07:22.574 --> 00:07:23.809
-Oh, of course.

00:07:23.809 --> 00:07:27.379
Of course, because
if there's a poetic element

00:07:27.379 --> 00:07:29.515
to the writing,
you immediately go to that.

00:07:29.515 --> 00:07:31.350
And that's why
Shakespeare is so great,

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.752
because it's structured
so wonderfully well.

00:07:33.752 --> 00:07:36.355
I mean, free prose
is very hard to learn,

00:07:36.355 --> 00:07:38.590
especially a lot
that's interrupted,

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:40.860
when you're doing
interrupted lines

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:43.296
and it's not necessary.

00:07:43.296 --> 00:07:45.731
But free prose is difficult.

00:07:45.731 --> 00:07:47.066
But Shakespeare,

00:07:47.066 --> 00:07:49.802
anything that's got a poetic
bent to it, it's helpful.

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:52.204
-It's just wonderful
to have you teaching.

00:07:52.204 --> 00:07:53.472
It's wonderful to have you
here in person.

00:07:53.472 --> 00:07:55.274
-Lovely to be here.
-Brian Cox, everybody!

00:07:55.274 --> 00:07:57.576
His acting tutorials
are available on BBC Maestro.

00:07:57.576 --> 00:07:59.912
We'll be right back
with Da'Vine Joy Randolph.

